# Where the tarball beside this file came from (#98). Update all three lines together on a bump,
# then re-run `make seed`.
#
# BUMP AT A MILESTONE, NOT AT EVERY TAG (ADR-0005). The app releases on its own cadence; gestion
# pins the version it decided to ship, so this file moves when a gestion release wants it to, not
# when the app tags.
#
# THIS ONE IS BUILT, NOT DOWNLOADED — it is our own app (ADR-0003, the IntraVox fork: upstream
# voxcloud/intravox, AGPL-3, modified in-repo per doctrine). `url=` names the fork tag it was
# built from. The build, run from the org-root twin (any clone of the repo works — the archive
# comes from the tag):
#
#     git -C IntraVox archive --format=tar --prefix=intravox/ <tag> \
#       | gzip -n9 > provisioning/apps/intravox/intravox-<version>.tar.gz
#
# `--prefix=intravox/` because ensure_vendored_app unpacks into custom_apps/ and Nextcloud needs
# the app id as the top directory. `gzip -n` keeps the mtime out of the header, so rebuilding the
# same tag yields the same bytes and the sha256 below stays checkable.
#
# TAG AFTER THE REBUILD, not before — this release is the proof: slugifyHeading's fold change
# lives in src/, but production runs the built js/. The v3.1.0-aps1 bundle carries the shared
# fold (measured: \p{Diacritic} present in js/intravox-main.js, absent in the pre-rebuild one);
# a tag cut before the rebuild would have shipped the private regex forever. The app's own gates
# (12 prebuild checks incl. check-aps-parity) ran green before the tag.
#
# 46 MB, 1546 files — the heavyweight of the vendored set (Talk is 52). The rider is upstream's
# own shipping shape (l10n machine translations, demo-data, showcases, the 3.3 MB vendors
# bundle); .gitattributes export-ignores already prune screenshots/internal docs. Recorded, not
# hidden. First place to look if this ever has to shrink.
#
# ONE tarball in this directory, ever: ensure_vendored_app globs *.tar.gz and fails on anything
# other than exactly one. A bump deletes the previous file, it does not sit beside it.

